<Project>
    <PropertyGroup>
        <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- Core Critter Stack dependencies. JasperFx and Weasel move in lockstep: Weasel 9.23.x
             carries a JasperFx 2.37.x floor, so bump both together rather than letting one resolve
             transitively. -->
        <!-- JasperFx 2.75.0 (from 2.74.0).

             THE BUMP IS THE WORK, and almost all of it is jasperfx#898/#899 — conjoined DOCUMENT
             tenancy, which the shared library had no seam for at all until now. One new suite
             (DocumentConjoinedTenancyCompliance, 10 facts) and seven more facts on
             ConjoinedEventTenancyCompliance, which had eleven. What Fisher needed for them:

             NOT AUTOMATIC — the new suite is RED on a correct store without the IDocumentStore change
             beside it. IDocumentSessionFactory gained LightweightSession(string) / QuerySession(string)
             additively, with THROWING defaults, and Fisher's own members take `string? tenantId = null`
             — which satisfies neither the parameterless member (an optional parameter does not) nor the
             string one (the nullability differs, and a hiding declaration on a derived interface never
             implements the base's anyway). So four explicit forwarders, same near-miss
             IDocumentReadOperations.Events and IDocumentSessionOperations.PendingStreams already carry.
             Delete them and Fisher still compiles, is still perfectly correct about tenancy, and fails
             every fact in the suite with NotSupportedException.

             ALSO NOT AUTOMATIC:
             jasperfx#888 — a tenant-aware IEventStore.OpenReadOnlyEventStore(tenantId), whose default
             throws for any non-null tenant. Implemented (DocumentStore.EventStore.cs), because the
             tier already opens a session per read precisely so it holds none: the tenant goes on the
             session and every read it serves is scoped by the same code that scopes an application's.
             Without it the whole read-only tier is unreachable on a store whose default tenant is
             refused. Closes a bullet of fisher#335.

             ONE RED FACT, and it is the SUITE's rather than Fisher's:
             DocumentConjoinedTenancyCompliance.optimistic_concurrency_is_scoped_to_the_tenant_for_a_shared_id
             cannot pass on any store that satisfies
             GuidOptimisticConcurrencyCompliance.a_successful_write_moves_the_instances_own_version_on
             — the two shared facts contradict each other. jasperfx#903; HANDOFF's red list has it, and
             tenant_scoped_concurrency_guards pins the behaviour it is about so a real regression cannot
             hide behind an already-failing fact.

             INHERITED, no Fisher change needed:
             jasperfx#889 — the source-generated DetermineAction decides per BATCH rather than per
             event. Fisher's projection dispatch is that generator's output, so this arrives whole; the
             projection suites are green unedited, which is what says so.
             jasperfx#890/#895/#896 — a marker in every generated assembly, exactly one copy of the
             generator handed to the compiler, and an opt-in JasperFxEventsRequireSourceGenerator build
             assertion. #895 matters to Fisher specifically: fisher#73 bundles the generator into the
             nupkg as an analyzer, which is the shape that could hand a consumer two copies, and the
             CS0433 it caused is now upstream's problem rather than the consumer's. Opting into #896 is
             fisher#337, not folded in — it is a build-time contract, not a bump.
             jasperfx#886/#897 — compliance fact wording. -->

        <!-- JasperFx 2.74.0 (from 2.72.0, spanning 2.73.0/.1/.2).

             NOT AUTOMATIC — this bump is RED without the fisher#307 change beside it:
             jasperfx#871/#878 lifted a canonical ArchivedStreamException into JasperFx.Events, and
             StreamArchivingCompliance now asserts THAT type rather than merely that the commit
             failed. So Fisher.Exceptions.ArchivedStreamException has to subclass it, and the two
             land together rather than as a bump and a follow-up. Worth knowing which way the lift
             went: it took FISHER's type as the canonical shape — named for the condition, carrying
             the id, naming the remedy — over Marten's generic InvalidStreamOperationException and
             Polecat's message-matching InvalidStreamException.

             UNBLOCKS, consumed separately:
             jasperfx#858/#862 — a store-agnostic JasperFx.Events.StubEventStream<T>. Fisher's
             IEventStream<T> IS the JasperFx type, so the stub satisfies it with no Fisher code at
             all; fisher#302 is the docs page that uses it.

             ONE SOURCE BREAK, and it is a name collision rather than a behaviour change:
             jasperfx#882 lifted a canonical DisabledTenantException into JasperFx.MultiTenancy, and
             Fisher has had one of its own since fisher#58. Any file importing both namespaces is
             CS0104 — three lines in tenant_registry.cs here, qualified rather than papered over with
             an alias so the collision stays visible. Adopting it the way fisher#307 adopts the
             archiving one is fisher#321, deliberately not folded in: that is an inheritance decision
             about a public type, not a bump.

             INHERITED, no Fisher change needed:
             jasperfx#879 — the canonical stream exception messages name a remedy. Fisher's own
             wording is unaffected (each of its subclasses goes through the message-overriding
             constructor precisely so an upstream rewording cannot move it), and every Fisher
             exception test still passes unedited, which is what says the protection works.
             jasperfx#881 — StaticTenantSource throws UnknownTenantIdException. Fisher supplies its
             own ITenantSource implementations, so this is inert here; fisher#309 already put Fisher's
             UnknownTenantException on that shared base.
             jasperfx#859/#861/#863 — an Event Model source disagreement names the source rather than
             only the rung, and EventModelClaim's two-argument constructor is restored. Fisher
             registers a ProjectionEventModelSource, so it gets the better diagnostic for free.

             INERT:
             jasperfx#884 (RuntimeCompiler's CodeGenerationException) is about runtime code
             generation, which Fisher does not do — projection dispatch is source-generated.
             jasperfx#880 is XML comments on AutoCreate. jasperfx#867 is a Wolverine codegen fix and
             jasperfx#865/#866 are the publish pipeline. -->
        <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx" Version="2.75.0" />
        <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx.Events" Version="2.75.0" />
        <!-- The shared cross-store event sourcing compliance suites. Source-only package: the
             suites compile inside Fisher.Tests so JasperFx's aggregate source generator binds
             Fisher's own session types. See src/Fisher.Tests/Compliance. -->
        <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx.Events.ComplianceTests" Version="2.75.0" />
        <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx.Events.SourceGenerator" Version="2.75.0" />
        <!-- Fisher.Tests only: the Microsoft.Extensions.AI adapter to IEmbeddingProvider, so the
             vector projection page's provider-wiring sample compiles. Fisher itself never takes it. -->
        <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx.Events.MicrosoftExtensionsAI" Version="2.75.0" />

        <!-- Weasel.Sqlite: schema management, table definitions, migrations, PRAGMA-applying data
             source. Weasel.Storage: the dialect-neutral closed-shape document + event storage
             runtime extracted from Marten, which Fisher plugs a SQLite dialect into rather than
             hand-writing storage.

             9.31.0 is a real floor rather than a preference: it carries the lifts Fisher now
             consumes instead of its own copies — SystemTextJsonSerializer and the public
             StorageSerializerAdapter (weasel#555) and the generic ITransactionParticipant
             (weasel#561). It also carries weasel#574, which brings the shared flat-table
             DecrementMemberMap into line with fisher#183; Fisher does not use that DSL yet, but the
             agreement is what makes adopting it later cost nothing.

             9.35.1 (from 9.32.0, spanning 9.33.0/9.34.0/9.35.0) is a message-and-diagnostics wave,
             inherited whole with no Fisher change. Nothing in it is a behaviour change Fisher has to
             answer, and the whole suite is green unedited.

             Reaching Fisher:
             weasel#595 — a numeric-revision ConcurrencyException always carries the UpdateRevision
             hint. Fisher's "strictly greater" rule (fisher#228) is the one an application meets by
             re-storing an instance that still carries its loaded revision, and the message now names
             the remedy rather than only the failure. Met while writing
             tenant_scoped_concurrency_guards, which is as good a demonstration as a test.
             weasel#596 — the per-event append gets the exception-transform hook the stream insert
             already had. Fisher's FisherQuickAppendEventsOperation is an IExceptionTransform, so both
             halves of an append now translate rather than only one.
             weasel#601/#602 — the assert path always throws DatabaseValidationException, and six
             user-facing message defects. Fisher's AssertDatabaseMatchesConfigurationAsync and
             AssertDatabaseMatchesConfigurationOnStartup (fisher#172) are the callers.
             weasel#597/#600 — the AutoCreate table is corrected, and AutoCreate.All warns before it
             drops and recreates an object. Fisher honours AutoCreate.None on its own on-demand paths
             (fisher#81), so this is the other end of the same setting.

             INERT: weasel#593/#620/#624 (sqlcmd and GO lines), #598/#599/#616 (SQL Server
             permissions and sp_getapplock), #594/#614 (Postgres concurrent index builds and the
             advisory lock), and #615/#621/#625/#627/#628/#629 (Weasel.EntityFrameworkCore, which
             Fisher.EntityFrameworkCore does not reference — it takes
             Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational alone). -->
        <PackageVersion Include="Weasel.Sqlite" Version="9.35.1" />
        <PackageVersion Include="Weasel.Storage" Version="9.35.1" />

        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.9" />
        <!-- Override the transitive SQLitePCLRaw 2.1.11 (vulnerable native lib,
             GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q) with 3.0.3, which ships the patched SQLite 3.50.x build. That
             build is also what puts JSONB and the json1 functions Fisher's storage relies on
             unconditionally in the box. -->
        <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="3.0.3" />

        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.0" />
        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.0" />
        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.0" />
        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="10.0.0" />
        <PackageVersion Include="Polly.Core" Version="8.6.5" />

        <!-- EF Core, for Fisher.EntityFrameworkCore. Pinned to the 9.x line rather than 10.x
             because the package multi-targets net9.0 and net10.0 as everything here does, and EF
             Core 10 is net10-only. 9.x targets net8.0, so it loads on both. Relational only in the
             package itself — the provider is the application's choice, and forcing
             Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite on a consumer would be Fisher deciding which EF
             provider their DbContext uses. The tests reference the Sqlite provider because a test
             needs a real one. -->
        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="9.0.14" />
        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="9.0.14" />

        <!-- Benchmarking (Fisher.Benchmarks only) -->
        <PackageVersion Include="BenchmarkDotNet" Version="0.15.8" />

        <!-- Testing -->
        <PackageVersion Include="NSubstitute" Version="5.3.0" />
        <PackageVersion Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />
        <!--
          Microsoft Testing Platform extensions, replacing coverlet.collector and the TRX logger,
          both of which are VSTest-only.

          BOTH ARE PINNED TO THE MTP 1.x LINE, NOT THE LATEST. xunit.v3 3.2.2 is built against
          Microsoft.Testing.Platform 1.x — its packages are literally named xunit.v3.core.mtp-v1.
          The 2.x releases of these extensions drag the platform assembly up to 2.x while
          Microsoft.Testing.Platform.MSBuild stays behind, and every test run then dies at startup
          with a TypeLoadException on IDataConsumer. 1.9.1 and 18.0.6 are the last MTP 1.x
          releases; move them forward only when xunit.v3 ships an mtp-v2 build.
        -->
        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.CodeCoverage" Version="18.0.6" />
        <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.TrxReport" Version="1.9.1" />
        <!-- Test projects reference xunit.v3; Fisher.TestUtils, a plain library that only authors
             custom attributes, references xunit.v3.extensibility.core instead — xunit.v3 forces
             OutputType=Exe onto whatever references it, which is wrong for a library. -->
        <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
        <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3.extensibility.core" Version="3.2.2" />
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
